TI  - An electrochemical biosensor based on phage-encoded protein RBP 41 for rapid and sensitive detection of Salmonella.
AB  - Salmonellosis caused by Salmonella contaminated food poses a serious threat to human health. The rapid and accurate detection of Salmonella is critical for preventing foodborne illness outbreaks. In this study, an electrochemical biosensor was developed using a newly identified biorecognition element, RBP 41, which is capable of specifically recognizing and binding to Salmonella. The biosensor was constructed through a layer-by-layer assembly of graphene oxide (GO), gold nanoparticles (GNPs), and RBP 41 on a glassy carbon electrode (GCE), with the GNPs amplifying the detection signal. The established biosensor was able to detect Salmonella in concentrations ranging from 3 to 106 CFU/mL within approximately 30 min by using differential pulse voltammetry (DPV) signal, and the estimated detection limit was to be 0.2984 Log10 CFU/mL. The biosensor demonstrated excellent specificity and was effective in detecting Salmonella in food matrices, such as skim milk and lettuce. Overall, this study highlights the potential of phage tail receptor binding proteins in biosensing and the proposed biosensor as a promising alternative for rapid and sensitive Salmonella detection in various samples.
