TI  - Multi-resistant organisms in burn patients: an end or a new beginning.
AB  - Infections are a major cause of morbidity and mortality in burn patients, and the rise of multidrug-resistant organisms (MDROs) has made it more challenging to manage and prevent infections. This review examines the available treatment options for MDROs in burn patients and anticipates the future challenges posed by their increasing prevalence. The review covers new antibiotics, such as Eravacycline and Plazomicin, as well as non-antibiotic therapies, such as bacteriophages and nanoparticles. Future research should focus on examining the long-term efficacy, cost-effectiveness, and in vivo efficacy of different treatment modalities. The potential of alternative therapies, such as probiotics and low-frequency magnetic fields, should also be explored. Accurate and rapid diagnostic and monitoring tools for detecting MDROs in burn patients should be developed. The emergence of MDROs in burn care is a challenge and a new beginning in infection innovation and novel treatments.
